ent performed action: 0.\n* On Round 2, you performed action: 0.\n* On Round 3, your opponent performed action: 0.\nYour valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nYou hold **Queen of Clubs, King of Hearts**.  \nThe board is: **7♦, K♣, J♣, 6♣, 4♥**.\n\nLet’s analyze your current hand:\n\n- You have **King of Hearts** and **Queen of Clubs**.\n- Paired with the **K♣** on the board → you have **a pair of Kings**, with **Queen kicker**.\n- The board shows four clubs: **K♣, J♣, 6♣, and 7♦, 4♥** — so there are **three clubs on board**: K♣, J♣, 6♣ → that's **three to a flush**.\n- Possible straight draws: The board has **7, 6, J** — which gives an open-ended straight draw possibility (9,8 or Q,10). Since you hold **Q**, any **10** would complete a straight (J-Q-K-A-T? No A. But 10-J-Q-K-7? Not consecutive). Let's check:\n  - Straight possibilities: **10 and 9** would make 7-8-9-10-J, but we don’t have those.\n  - With Q and K, the sequence **10-J-Q-K-A** is broadway, but no A or 10 on board.\n  - However, **T-9** would give others a straight, but you don’t complete it unless you had T-9.\n  - Your Q helps: **Q-K-A-J-T** isn't valid. But **J-Q-K** is just high cards.\n  - So, **you do not have a straight**, but you do have **top pair (Kings)** with **second-nut kicker (Queen)** — only King-high would beat you if someone has Ace-King.\n\nBut wait — **possible flush on board?** There are **three clubs**: K♣, J♣, 6♣ → so a **flush is possible** if someone has two more clubs.\n\nAlso, note: **Jack of Clubs and Queen of Clubs** — if someone has **10♣ and 9♣**, they have a straight flush draw, but unlikely.\n\nNow, what hands could beat you?\n\n- Any **two pair**? Board doesn’t pair, so two pair must come from hole cards (e.g., J+6, 7+6, etc.) — possible but weak.\n- **Sets**: Someone with 7-7, 6-6, or 4-4 would have a set.\n- **Flush**: If opponent has two club cards (especially higher than yours), they could have a flush. For example, **A♣ + x** would beat your pair.\n- **Straight**: As said, 9-10 of any suit makes 7-8-9-10-J → but 8 missing. No 8 on board. So no straight completed. Closest is open-ended if 9-10 comes, but river already dealt — this is **post-river**.\n\nWait — all five community cards are out: **7♦, K♣, J♣, 6♣, 4♥** → this is **the River round (Round 3)**.\n\nSo no more cards coming.\n\nYour current hand: **Pair of Kings**, with **Queen kicker**. Also, you have **Queen of Clubs** — so you have **four clubs**: Q♣ in hand, plus K♣, J♣, 6♣ on board → that’s **four to a flush**.\n\nBut you only have **one club in hand (Q♣)**, and the other card is **K♥** — so yes, you have **four clubs** between hand and board.\n\nWait: Your hand: **Q♣, K♥**\n\nBoard: **7♦, K♣, J♣, 6♣, 4♥**\n\nSo your suited cards: Q♣, K♣, J♣, 6♣ → that’s **four clubs** — you need five for a flush.\n\nYou **do not have a flush**.\n\nYou have **a pair of Kings** (K♥ and K♣), with **Q♣ kicker**.\n\nIs there a better pair?\n\nCould someone have **Ace-King**? Then they’d have **Kings with Ace kicker** — which beats your **King-Queen**.\n\nOr **King-Jack**? Then same pair, but kicker is Jack — you have Queen, so you beat that.\n\nSo your hand strength: **Top pair, top kicker (Queen)** — decent, but vulnerable.\n\nNow, possible made flushes: three clubs on board — so if opponent has any two clubs, especially **A♣x♣, 10♣9♣, 10♣8♣, 9♣8♣, etc.**, they have a flush.\n\nGiven that opponent has been betting aggressively earlier:\n\n- Round 0: You raised 200 → they called (0)\n- Round 1: They raised 100 → you raised 200 → they raised 400 → you called\n- Rounds 2 and 3: both checked\n\nThis suggests they may have **slowed down** on later streets — possibly indicating weakness or a trap.\n\nBut their initial aggression on flop/turn might suggest strength.\n\nNow on river, they **checked** — which could mean:\n\n- They missed a draw and